9-11-2026 DTO, Validation and API Contracts

DTO
    - Act as a contract between your API and its consumers
    - expose only what's needed
    - loose coupling - stable contract, decouple API from internal models
    - better performance - smaller payloads
    - easier evolution and versioning

Entity vs DTO (slide 6)
    - Entity: represents the data model in your application, often tied to the database schema
    - DTO: communicates data safely and efficiently

Request DTO Design (slide 10)
    - captures the data sent by the client for a specific operation, validates input and protects your domain model
    - client supplies just what it's allowed to set
    - Validation-ready, annotate fields with Jakarta validation constraints
        - eg @NotBlank String fullName
    - use separate create, replace, and patch contracts when their required fields or semantics differ
    - over-posting protection, never bind server-controlled or internal fields with client input

Response DTO Design (slide 12)
    - defines the data returned after a request is processed - consistent, secure, efficient

Mapping strategies (slide 15)
    - convert entities to DTOs (and back) using the right approach
        - Manual Mapping
        - ModelMapper
        - MapStruct
        - Legacy reflection tools